WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.212
Now we will create a small DataFrame directly in Python.

00:00:04.212 --> 00:00:09.067
In the code, we first import Pandas using the standard alias, pd.

00:00:09.067 --> 00:00:13.262
Then we pass a list of dictionaries into pd.DataFrame.

00:00:13.262 --> 00:00:15.247
Each dictionary represents one row.

00:00:15.247 --> 00:00:21.602
The dictionary keys become the column names, and the values become the data stored in that row.

00:00:21.602 --> 00:00:24.967
Click Run and look at the result.

00:00:24.967 --> 00:00:30.082
You should see five order records with order ID, channel, and amount.

00:00:30.082 --> 00:00:36.552
This small example is useful because it lets us practice Pandas without depending on an external file.

00:00:36.552 --> 00:00:41.182
Notice that the rows automatically receive an index starting at zero.

00:00:41.182 --> 00:00:44.377
The index is not the same thing as the order ID.

00:00:44.377 --> 00:00:50.182
It is Pandas' label for the rows unless we explicitly change it.

00:00:50.182 --> 00:00:55.547
Try changing one amount or adding another order to the list, then run the code again.

00:00:55.547 --> 00:01:00.732
The point of this slide is not the specific numbers.

00:01:00.732 --> 00:01:06.067
It is to understand how a collection of Python records becomes a structured DataFrame that we can inspect

00:01:06.067 --> 00:01:08.054
and analyze.
